  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThrough Thursday: Widespread Rainfall\n\nWidespread light to moderate showers have overspread much of the \nUpper and Mid Mississippi River Valleys overnight with QPE totals on \nthe low side generally at or below 0.25 inches. This activity is \nforming within an axis of increased mid-level moisture with \nsynoptic support from strong southwest flow aloft ahead of an \nupper low/trough across the Desert Southwest. Totals through \nthe overnight hours and into mid morning may reach 0.5-0.75, \nmainly for areas south of the IA/MN border where some heavier \nreturns are showing up on radar. Heavier rainfall expected later\nin the day with limited instability as pWats increase overnight\nand through the day tomorrow from strong 850 mb moisture \ntransport into the region. \n\nAn break in rainfall is expected for much of the area before\nsubsequent, likely heavier, rainfall forms during the afternoon,\ncontinuing into early Thursday morning. As mentioned earlier, \nwith strong 850 mb moisture transport into the region, pWats \nwill likely climb to 1.75+ inches, potentially approaching 2\" \nacross our far southern counties (Grant/Clayton) (30.00Z HREF).\nSimilar to previous forecasts, the overall axis for heaviest \nrainfall continues to shift south into Central Iowa, aligning\nwith the overall trend in the EC ensemble for many days now. \nTherefore, current forecast confidence for locally heaviest \nrainfall looks to be limited to far southern portions of \nSouthwest Wisconsin (Grant and potentially Crawford/Richland \nCounties) and far southern portions of Northeast Iowa (Fayette \nand Clayton Counties) with less than 1\" likely elsewhere locally\nby Friday morning. \n\nNBM 30.01Z 1\"+ rainfall probabilities from 60 to 80% in the \naforementioned counties appear slightly earlier with a sharp \ngradient/cutoff to the north. LREF 29.12Z has a more broad area\nacross northeast Iowa and Southwest Wisconsin for its \nprobability of 1 inch of rainfall or greater (40-90%) with mean \ntotals generally in the 0.5-2 inch range across the region. With\nthe southern shift in heavy rainfall, the flooding threat is \nstarting to dwindle, especially for area rivers. 90-95th \npercentile amounts from this system looked to be in the 2.5-4.5 \ninch range and this would likely create some areal and river \nflooding concerns but this concern seems to be diminishing for \nour local area. A cold front is expected to move in through the \nday on Thursday which will start to usher in a drier airmass. It\nwill make slow progress through the region as high pressure \nbuilds in behind it with rain slowly ending from northwest to \nsoutheast late Thursday night. \n\nFriday into Early Next Week: Mostly Dry and Trending Cooler\n\nThe H300 jet stream is forecast to sit across southern Canada and \nmay dig south at times into the Great Lakes region to northeastern \nUS this weekend into early next week. With a cold front south of the \nlocal forecast area and some cooler air filtering in due to the \ntroughing, daily high temperatures will be a few degrees cooler into \nearly next week. The cooler air mass will be more noticeable at\nnight with lows dropping into the 40s to 30s. As of now, Monday\nmorning looks to be coolest morning over the next week with the\ncoldest temperatures potentially approaching freezing across \ncentral Wisconsin. Mostly dry conditions are expected during \nthis time frame with the exception being on Saturday as there \nare low chances for light rain showers over the northern half of\nthe CWA.",
